[Line "Chinese Dragon: 10...Rb8"]
[Section "9.Bc4: other systems for Black"]
[Summary "Black supports ...b5 with the rook and aims to open the b-file with ...Na5-c4. It avoids the 12.Kb1 lines, and after 12.Bh6 Black defends with the unusual ...e5."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. Bc4 Bd7 10. O-O-O
Rb8 { [%cal Gb7b5,Gc6a5,Ga5c4] The Chinese Dragon. The rook goes to the b-file instead of the c-file:
...b5 is supported, and after ...Na5-c4 and Bxc4 Black recaptures with the b-pawn and has an open file
against b2. It is a way to play the 9.Bc4 lines without meeting 11...Ne5 12.Kb1. }
11. Bb3 { The bishop retreats before ...b5 arrives with tempo. }
( 11. Nxc6? { Opening the b-file for a rook that is already on it. } 11... bxc6 12. h4 Qa5 { [%cal
Gb8b2,Gf6e4] Black is already attacking. Jones won a miniature here when White played 13.Kb1, allowing
13...Nxe4 with a mating threat on b2 that costs the queen. } )
( 11. h4 { The other move order. } 11... b5 12. Bb3 Na5 { This is the note with 12.h4 below. } )
11... Na5 { [%cal Ga5c4,Ga5b3] Threatening ...Nc4, and keeping ...Nxb3 in hand for the moment the bishop
becomes dangerous. } 12. Bh6 { The most topical move: before the b-file opens White removes the bishop
that would use it. }
( 12. h4 { The original main line. } 12... b5 13. h5 Nc4 14. Bxc4 bxc4 { [%cal Gb8b2,Gd8b6] Black has
what the opening was played for. } 15. hxg6 fxg6 16. Bh6 Qb6 { Forcing a weakness, since b2 is attacked
twice. } 17. b3 Bxh6 18. Qxh6 Rf7 { The rook covers h7 along the rank and the king is safe. Black has the
c- and b-files and the better pawn cover, and has won most of the elite games from here. } )
12... Bxh6 13. Qxh6 b5 { [%cal Gb5b4] The queen has been drawn to h6, so Black can advance without
worrying about b2 for the moment. } 14. h4 { [%cal Rh4h5] } 14... e5 { [%csl Rd6] A move Dragon players
are taught never to make, and here it is the point. The knight has to leave d4, where it supported Nd5
and the sacrifices on f5 and e6, and the seventh rank is opened for the defence. The hole on d6 does not
matter: the dark-squared bishops are gone and White's pieces are committed to the kingside. } 15. Nde2
b4 16. Nd5 Nxb3+ { Only now, when the recapture has to be made with the a-pawn. } 17. axb3 Nxd5 18. Rxd5
Rb6 { [%cal Gb6d6] Covering d6 along the rank. } 19. h5 g5 { **Verdict:** Black is fine. The h-file
stays closed and the queen on h6 is cut off from the game. If White takes on d6, Black answers ...f6 and
has won every elite game played; otherwise ...f6 and ...Rf7 secure the king and Black turns to the
weakened queenside. The Chinese
Dragon is a serious system with far less theory than the main lines, and a good second weapon. } *

[Line "The old main line: 10...Qa5"]
[Section "9.Bc4: other systems for Black"]
[Summary "The queen comes to a5 and both rooks to the c-file. For decades this was the Dragon; 12.Kb1 took the sting out of the queen sortie and it is now a sideline."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. Bc4 Bd7 10. O-O-O
Qa5 { [%cal Gf8c8,Gc6e5,Ge5c4] The classical set-up, the one Chris Ward's books taught: queen to a5,
king's rook to c8, knight to e5 and c4. Everything points at c3 and a2. } 11. Bb3 Rfc8 { The king's rook,
so that the other can stay on a8 behind the a-pawn or go to b8. The drawback is that f8 is no longer
available to the king's defence. } 12. Kb1 { [%csl Yb1] The move that put the line out of fashion. With
the king on b1, ...Qxd2 is not check, so every Nd5 has to be reckoned with; a2 is covered; and the queen
on a5 is more of a target than an attacker. The old main line 12.h4 Ne5 13.Kb1 reaches the same
position as the game. } 12... Ne5 13. h4 { The move with White's best results in practice is 13.Bg5,
pinning the knight so that Nd5 becomes a real threat; Black answers 13...Nc4. The simple 13.Nd5 Qxd2
leads to an ending that Black holds without trouble. } 13... Nc4
( 13... b5 { The other plan: after ...Nc4 Black wants to recapture with the b-pawn and use the b-file, as
in the Chinese Dragon. It has scored at least as well as the main line. } )
14. Bxc4 Rxc4 15. Nb3 { [%cal Rb3a5] Chasing the queen and covering d4 and a1. } 15... Qc7 16. Bd4 { The
bishop blocks the fourth rank and offers the exchange of Black's best piece. } 16... Be6 { [%cal
Ga7a5,Ga5a4] Clearing the queen's path and preparing to chase the knight. } 17. h5 a5 { **Verdict:**
playable. A typical position of the old theory: White has the h-file, Black drives the knight from b3
with ...a4 and breaks through to c2 and b2, and exchange sacrifices on c3 are always in the air. Black
has done well enough from here in practice. The reason 10...Qa5 is rare today lies earlier, at moves
12 and 13, where White has a choice of promising lines and Black does not. } *

[Line "Giri's 9...Nxd4 and 10...Be6"]
[Section "9.Bc4: other systems for Black"]
[Summary "Black exchanges on d4 at once and challenges the bishop on c4. An old line that Giri has rehabilitated: it avoids the main-line theory and often leads to an ending in which Black has no problems."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. Bc4 Nxd4 10. Bxd4 {
10.Qxd4 loses the bishop pair to 10...Ng4. } 10... Be6 { [%cal Gd8a5,Gb7b5] Giri's main recommendation
against 9.Bc4. The pieces go straight to their most active squares and the queenside play starts
without ...Bd7 and ...Rc8. White must either exchange on e6 or retreat and allow ...Qa5 and ...b5. }
11. Bb3 { Keeping the tension. }
( 11. Bxe6 { This doubles Black's pawns and gives him the d5-square for good: with a pawn on e6 the
knight on c3 has nowhere to go. } 11... fxe6 12. O-O-O Qa5 13. Rhe1 { [%cal Re4e5] The critical move:
White forgets the h-pawn and plays e5. }
( 13. Kb1 Rac8 14. h4 Nh5 { Blocking the h-pawn; Giri also likes 14...Nd7, meeting h5 with ...g5. }
15. Bxg7 Kxg7 { White's kingside play is stopped, since g4 is met by ...Nf4 or ...Ng3, and Black doubles
on the c-file. } )
13... Nd7 { The knight stays near the centre when White is not playing for h5. } 14. Bxg7 Kxg7 15. e5 d5
{ Keeping the centre closed: 15...Nxe5 16.f4 would return the pawn and give the knight on c3 the d5
square. Black now steers for an ending with ...Rac8 and an exchange of queens on d2, and regroups with
...Kf7 and ...Nb8-c6 to challenge a knight on d4. Giri's verdict is that neither side can make
progress. } )
11... Qa5 { [%cal Ga5a2,Ga5c3] The queen controls b5 and d5 from here and presses on c3 and a2. } 12. O-O-O
b5 { [%cal Gb5b4] The new twist. The older 12...Rfc8 13.Kb1 b5 allows 14.a3, stopping ...b4; played at
once, the pawn gets there. } 13. Kb1 { [%cal Rc3d5] The usual precaution when the queens face each other
on d2 and a5: Nd5 now threatens to answer ...Qxd2 with the in-between check on e7. } 13... b4 14. Nd5
( 14. Ne2 { Passive: the knight gives up d5 and a4. } 14... Bxb3 15. cxb3 e5 { [%cal Gd6d5,Gf8d8] With
the knight on e2 Black can break in the centre: ...Rfd8 and ...d5 cannot be prevented. } )
14... Bxd5 { The bishop takes, keeping the knight that defends e7 and the kingside. } 15. exd5
( 15. Bxd5 { This looks strong, since the bishops come off on g7, but there is a tactical flaw. }
15... Nxd5 16. Bxg7 Nc3+! { The in-between check that Giri points out: the bishop on g7 is not going
anywhere, and first the knight opens the b-file. } 17. Bxc3
( 17. bxc3 Rfb8 { The threats on the b-file win the piece back with interest: 18.cxb4 Rxb4+ 19.Qxb4
Qxb4+ 20.Bb2 Rb8. } )
17... bxc3 18. Qxc3 { After the queens come off Black regains the pawn against White's ruined queenside,
and it is White who has to work for the draw in the rook ending. } )
15... Qb5 { [%cal Ga7a5,Ga5a4] The queen makes way for the a-pawn and offers an exchange on d3 or e2. }
16. Rhe1
( 16. Qd3 Qxd3 17. Rxd3 Nd7 { Trading the bishop that eyes g7 and a7. } 18. Bxg7 Kxg7 19. Re3 Kf6 { The
king defends e7 himself. The ending is equal, and in practice a little easier for Black. } )
16... a5 17. Qe2 Qxe2 18. Rxe2 a4 { Gaining space with tempo. } 19. Bc4 Rfc8 20. b3 Rc7 { **Verdict:**
equal. Black covers e7 along the rank, the king goes to f8, and the knight has d7, b6 and c5 to choose
from. White has the bishop pair and Black the sounder pawns and a target on d5; the many elite games
from here have slightly favoured Black.

This is the practical alternative to the Topalov and Soltis lines: far less to remember, and positions
in which the Dragon's structure is all Black needs. } *
